  "response": "# Refactor plan: `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`\n\n## What the finding says (crime_00035, `svg_with_embedded_raster`, high severity, confidence 0.95)\n\n- **Number of embedded rasters: 2.** The evidence reads: \"2 `<image href=\\\"data:image/…;base64,…\\\">` occurrences\".\n- **MIME types: `image/jpeg`, `image/png`.** The evidence reads: \"embedded MIME types: image/jpeg, image/png\".\n- **Size:** \"SVG byte size: 592 bytes (includes the base64 overhead)\".\n- **Suggested fix:** \"re-author the raster region as vector paths, or split it out to its own asset\".\n\n## What's in the file\n\nI read the file to see how the two rasters are laid out:\n\n```xml\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"200\" height=\"80\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 80\">\n  <title>Partner logo</title>\n  <image x=\"0\"   y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" href=\"data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0…\"/>\n  <image x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" xlink:href=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,/9j/4AAQ…\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThere are two problems the scan doesn't mention:\n\n1. **Both payloads are 1×1-pixel images**, stretched to fill 80×80 boxes. The decoded PNG and JPEG headers are both 1×1. So the file holds no usable artwork to trace. The real vector shapes have to come from the partner's brand kit or the original design file (Figma, Illustrator, etc.).\n2. **The JPEG `<image>` uses `xlink:href`, but the file never declares `xmlns:xlink`.** That prefix is unbound, so strict XML parsers reject the file and some renderers drop that image. Any rewrite should use plain `href`, which SVG 2 supports.\n\n## Remediation steps\n\n### 1. Left slot, PNG at x=0 (80×80): redraw as vector paths\nPNG in a logo usually means a flat-colour mark or icon. That kind of shape should be `<path>`, `<rect>` or `<circle>` geometry, not pixels.\n- Get the partner's official SVG or EPS mark, or re-export the layer from the design tool as vector geometry with no \"embed image\" option.\n- Add the geometry inline inside `<g transform=\"translate(0,0)\">`, scaled to the 80×80 box.\n- Use flat `fill` values, or `<linearGradient>` if the mark has gradients. Don't use filters that rasterise.\n\n### 2. Right slot, JPEG at x=100 (80×80): depends on what it shows\nJPEG usually means photographic content.\n- **If it's actually a flat or wordmark element** (JPEG used by mistake), redraw it as vector paths the same way as step 1. Convert any text to outlines, or use `<text>` with a web-safe font stack. Then the SVG has no rasters at all, which is the goal.\n- **If it's truly photographic** (a texture or photo can't become paths), move it to its own file, for example `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.webp`. Export it at 2× the display size (160×160) so it stays sharp on high-DPI screens. Then reference it by URL:\n  ```xml\n  <image x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" href=\"partner-logo-photo.webp\"/>\n  ```\n  Note: an `<image>` with an external URL does not load when the SVG is used through `<img src>` or as a CSS background. In that case, compose the two parts in markup instead: the vector SVG plus an adjacent `<img>`.\n\n### 3. Target end state\n**Preferred: one self-contained vector file.**\n- `src/assets/partner-logo.svg` contains only `<path>`/`<g>` geometry and no `<image>` elements.\n- It keeps `viewBox=\"0 0 200 80\"` and the `<title>`.\n- The `width` and `height` attributes can go, so CSS controls the size.\n\n**Fallback, only if the right-hand element is truly photographic:**\n- `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`: the left mark as vector paths, plus one `<image href=\"partner-logo-photo.webp\">` that points at the external file.\n- `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.webp`: the standalone raster, with a `.png` copy if you need older browsers.\n\n### 4. Check the result\n- Run `grep -c 'data:image' src/assets/partner-logo.svg`. It should return `0`.\n- Run `crimes scan --format json` again and confirm the fingerprint `svg_with_embedded_raster::src/assets/partner-logo.svg::` is gone.\n- Look at the logo at 1×, 2× and 4× zoom to confirm the vector parts stay sharp.\n- Check that everything that imports the logo still points at `partner-logo.svg`. The filename doesn't change, so nothing downstream should break.\n\nThe file stays in place and keeps its name. Only its contents change from pixel placeholders to real geometry.",
